Practical NO: 2

Linux File System Navigation: Perform directory navigation and listening operations.

Theory: Linux provides a hierarchical file system for storing and organizing files and directories. 
Directory navigation commands help users move between different folders while listing commands help them view the contents of a directory.

Requirement:

•Computer system with Linux install.
•Linux Terminal
•User account with permission to create and remove directories
•Basic knowledge of Linux file system and commands

a) List files using ls, ls-l, ls-a

$ ls :- Display files and directories in the current directory.

$ ls -l :- Display files and directories in detailed format.

$ ls -a :- Display all files and directories including hidden files.

b) Change directories using cd (absolute & relative paths)

$ cd .. :- Moves to the parent directory.

$ cd directory_name :- Changes the specified directory using a relative path.

$ cd /home/user/directory_name :- Changes to the specified directory using an absolute path.

c) Create directories using mkdir

$ mkdir directory_name :- Create a new directory with the specified name.

d) Remove directories using rmdir

$ rmdir directory_name :- Removes an empty directory from the current directory.
